From: Erik Gonzalez <dev@gonzalezerik.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 15:03:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/15] bsps/powerpc/ibookg3: Correct the gem reset note

The reset failures came from the FreeBSD gem driver writing the reset
to the interrupt mask register.  Only the receiver and transmitter
reset messages remain on QEMU, whose sungem model does not clear those
reset bits.
---
 bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md | 15 ++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
index f5b000a0c4c..2e0ed1a653e 100644
--- a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
+++ b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
@@ -38,13 +38,14 @@ which selects BSD bus resource activation for the PCI space.
 Notes for QEMU
 --------------
 
-* QEMU's sungem model only performs the software reset side effect on
-  its private `GREG_SWRST` register at offset `0x1010`; the FreeBSD
-  driver's reset write to `GEM_RESET` (`0x0010`) lands in the backing
-  store without resetting, so those bits never self-clear and the
-  driver prints `cannot reset receiver/transmitter` while the device
-  still works. On real hardware `GEM_RESET` self-clears and the
-  messages do not appear.
+* The FreeBSD gem driver in rtems-libbsd `6-freebsd-14` writes its
+  software reset to offset `0x0010` (the interrupt mask) instead of the
+  reset register at `0x1010`, a FreeBSD 14 regression; the workbench's
+  libbsd branch fixes it. With the fix the full reset completes.
+* QEMU's sungem model does not clear the reset bit after a receiver-only
+  or transmitter-only reset (it does after a full reset), so the driver
+  still prints `cannot reset receiver` and `cannot reset transmitter` on
+  QEMU. The device works regardless; real hardware clears the bits.
 
 Memory layout: the image is linked to run at 16 MiB (`0x01000000`) with load
 addresses starting at zero, which is what QEMU's `mac99` `-kernel` loader
